Here is a simple C++ program that reads a text file line by line and counts the number of occurrences of a specific word in the file:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string filename, word, line;
    std::string searchWord;
    int count = 0;

    // Get the filename and the word to search for from user input
    std::cout << "Enter the filename: ";
    std::cin >> filename;
    std::cout << "Enter the word to search for: ";
    std::cin >> searchWord;

    // Open the file for reading
    std::ifstream file(filename);

    if (file.is_open()) {
        while (getline(file, line)) {
            // Reset string stream and find position of the search word in the line
            std::stringstream ss(line);
            std::string token;
            while (ss >> token) {
                if (token == searchWord) {
                    count++;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        // Close the file
        file.close();

        // Print the result
        std::cout << "The word '" << searchWord << "' occurs " << count << " times in the file.\n";
    } else {
        std::cerr << "Unable to open file: " << filename << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This program uses the `std::ifstream` class to open the file for reading, and the `std::getline()` function to read each line. It then uses a `std::stringstream` to tokenize each line into words and checks if the current word matches the search word. If a match is found, the counter is incremented. The program prints the result at the end.

A binary search is an efficient search algorithm that works on sorted arrays or lists. It repeatedly divides the search interval in half. If the value of the search key is less than the item in the middle of the interval, the algorithm continues on the lower half. Otherwise, it continues on the upper half. This process continues until the value is found or the interval is empty.

Here is the implementation of binary search algorithm in Python:

```python
def binary_search(arr, low, high, x):
    # Check base case
    if high >= low:
